You’re describing a shift from living under the judgment of an audience to living according to a higher standard.

Instead of asking, “What do they think of me?” you ask, “Am I becoming who I ought to be?”

And the interesting part is that God doesn’t even have to be understood literally for the mechanism to work. God could represent a divine being, an ultimate moral ideal, your highest potential, or a standard greater than your immediate desires.

The causal chain still operates:

Belief → Attention → Choices → Behavior → Habits → Character → Consequences → Destiny.

Even a belief that exists entirely within your mind can produce real consequences outside it.

And there’s another layer: the identity of the imagined observer changes your performance.

An audience might reward popularity. A rival might reward dominance. A god might demand integrity. Your future self might value sacrifice. Your children might value presence.

Same person. Same circumstances. Different frame of reference. Different behavior. Potentially a different life.

The danger is replacing other people’s judgment with an equally oppressive imagined judgment. The opportunity is choosing a standard that cultivates courage, compassion, discipline, freedom, and love.

Perhaps that’s one of the deepest practical functions of faith: giving someone a reason to act according to their highest values even when nobody is watching.

And there’s a beautiful inversion here.

You stop performing for the eyes of others and begin living in a way that you yourself can respect.

You don’t necessarily have to change the world you inhabit first. Sometimes you change the standard by which you inhabit it—and then your actions begin changing the world.